WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.197 --> 00:00:04.751
(audience chattering)

00:00:04.751 --> 00:00:07.751
(audience cheering)

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.250
- On behalf of the faculty and staff

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:25.860
of the Yale School of Public Health,

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:30.247
I congratulate the members
of the Class of 2022.

00:00:30.247 --> 00:00:33.247
(audience cheering)

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:40.470
We are gathered to
celebrate your achievements

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.690
and acknowledge this graduation milestone.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:47.870
My colleagues and I welcome,
thank, and congratulate all

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:51.670
who played a critical role
in shaping the aspirations

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.520
of all our graduates of the
Yale School of Public Health

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.100
and contributed to your success.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.620
These special people
extend well beyond Yale,

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.990
most notably including
your parents or guardians,

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:07.230
your role models, your
siblings, your grandparents,

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:10.100
your extended family and
friends, and, for some of you,

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.710
your spouses or partners
and your children.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.130
We want to thank all of
you who have made possible

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.620
the academic and life
journeys of our graduates.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.840
So, families and friends of our graduates,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.100
please accept our heartfelt
gratitude as we pause

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.830
to applaud your contributions
to our YSPH graduates.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.830
(audience cheering)

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.390
Now, I address our MPH graduates.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:55.750
The past 30 months have thrust our school

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:59.010
and our discipline of public
health into the spotlight.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.840
As we have striven to overcome

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.260
this historic pandemic threat,

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.220
our school's faculty have highlighted

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:09.310
the social inequities
that inhibit wellness

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:12.950
in the most vulnerable
members of our population.

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:16.260
These have included the
institutionalized elderly,

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.470
our frontline workers, our
health care workers in homes

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:25.170
and longterm care settings,
and clinics and hospitals.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.180
Special efforts are needed
for persons with disabilities,

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.240
those with social isolation,

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:34.610
those with housing, food,
or employment insecurity.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.700
All these persons have been highlighted

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.290
in your studies, and for many of you,

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:43.780
your experiences as you
studied here in New Haven.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:48.180
Whether for COVID-19, maternal
mortality, or injuries,

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.250
death rates confirm racial,
ethnic, and economic disparities

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:55.670
in both risk and access
to prevention and care.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:59.890
Our environmental scientists
work on global plastics,

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:04.790
toxins, climate change, and
safe air qualities in buildings.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:07.270
Our epidemiologists, biostatisticians,

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:10.330
and modelers have worked
to unearth the causes

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.020
and solutions of the COVID-19 pandemic

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:14.840
and to forecast its course,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.460
as they have done for TB,
HIV, vector-borne diseases,

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:24.020
and a host of other tropical
and local infections.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:27.160
Our social and behavioral
scientists have refined

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.700
health communications and messaging

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.280
for risk reduction and wellness,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.980
also examining the structural
determinants of illness.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.900
Our biologists have helped
discover new diagnostics,

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.210
new therapeutics and vaccines,

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:45.163
Unearthed some of the origins
of disease pathogenesis.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.830
Our policy and management
faculty have tackled a myriad

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.760
of systemic flaws in our health system,

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.930
issues in tobacco and substance use,

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.373
and vaccine policy, to name but a few.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.440
Our Office of Public Health Practice,

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.290
our Office of Communications,

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:07.280
our Office of Alumni and
Development have striven to engage

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.660
our various communities with new vigor,

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:13.600
helping transform our
relevance to our university,

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.090
our alumni, our city of New
Haven, our state and region,

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:20.330
and even at national and
international levels.

00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:22.280
Our Emerging Infections Program

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.340
and our Community Alliance
for Research and Engagement,

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:26.640
that is a full partnership

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.950
with Southern Connecticut
State University,

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:33.950
deserve credit for their
massive community service.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:39.010
Public health is more visible
than ever within society,

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:41.890
and we see the compelling
issues highlighted by media

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:45.193
and policymakers alike as never before.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.460
YSPH students, staff,
and faculty have worked

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.760
with nursing homes, school districts,

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.020
arts organizations, Yale
alumni, other schools at Yale,

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.820
and our partners in low
and middle-income countries

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.070
of Africa, Asia, and Latin America.

00:05:02.007 --> 00:05:03.950
We've engaged our city and our state,

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:05.980
as well as governments and institutions

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:08.200
in other states including New York,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.040
Rhode Island, even
Kentucky and California.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.480
Our strong community has
been on the forefront

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.690
of Yale's response to the pandemic

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:20.040
and continuing our momentum
in the many compelling themes

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.680
of health care reform,
informatics and data sciences,

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.070
control of chronic and
infectious diseases,

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.170
promoting healthy mothers and children,

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:31.750
seeking healthy aging paradigms,

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:35.690
and addressing LGBTQ health,
protecting our environment,

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:38.720
and adapting to change,
just to name a few.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.680
All of you graduating in
2022 have attended YSPH

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:46.860
in a historic moment for
our nation and our world.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.020
Our community embodies boldness of vision,

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:52.530
persistence in the face of obstacles,

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:55.470
and a commitment to public education

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.800
and welfare in its health challenges.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.060
The public health workforce,

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:01.940
into which most of you will enter,

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.440
works in society's shadows.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.990
After all, a patient assisted by a nurse

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:11.770
or doctor is fully aware of
the benefits that might ensue.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:14.610
But a community kept safe
by public health insight

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:16.940
and exertion may be quite unaware

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:20.020
of the hazards prevented or mitigated.

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:22.540
In this year of 2022,

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:25.000
you've equipped yourselves
for further study

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.270
or for immediate entry
into the health workforce

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:32.140
with skills that the world needs urgently.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:34.370
I have one hope for all of you,

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:37.480
that is that your career is such

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:42.420
that you wake up on Monday
morning to welcome the work week

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:45.450
for the ability to improve our society

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:47.563
and the world each and every working day.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:50.530
Despite constraints due to COVID-19,

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:53.470
I hope you leave with
pride in your alma mater,

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:55.630
knowing that your studies
in public health happened

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:58.860
at a truly historic time of challenge

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:02.853
for our university, society,
and global community.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:06.520
When people ask me about the best part

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.190
of my deanship since 2017,

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:11.937
I can say that the passion
of our students, staff,

00:07:11.937 --> 00:07:15.410
and faculty have motivated
and invigorated me

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:18.370
to strive to improve our
school and our disciplines.

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:22.220
Meeting our future public
health leaders, all of you,

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:23.720
has enabled me to learn about

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:27.040
your accomplishments,
plans, and aspirations.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:30.390
While our world has
its immense challenges,

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:33.970
I have a tremendous sense
of optimism for the future

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:38.150
of the field of public
health as we turn the mantle

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:42.040
of leadership over to
you, our new generation.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:45.070
I speak on behalf of all
the faculty and staff

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:47.960
when I say that we've been
honored to work with you,

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:51.113
guide your education, and learn from you.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:58.100
It now gives me great pleasure
to introduce Nandini Deo.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:04.170
Nandini has been chosen by her peers

00:08:06.251 --> 00:08:09.620
to deliver this year's student address.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.210
Nandini's passion lies in
community-based trauma prevention.

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.780
Prior to her time at YSPH,

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:21.108
she worked in federal and state HIV,

00:08:21.108 --> 00:08:23.930
STI, and TB prevention at the CDC,

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:27.050
the Oregon Health Authority

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:30.293
and in the Utah Department
of Health as well.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:33.123
During her time at YSPH,

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:37.390
Nandini has had the privilege of working

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:42.230
on the border identity stress
team led by Dr. Skyler Jackson

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:45.510
to capture the psychosocial
experiences of individuals

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.000
who defy binary identification

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.400
across race, gender,
and sexual orientation.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:54.890
She's also worked under the
guidance of Dr. Keitra Thompson

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:58.800
as a qualitative evaluator
for the MOMS partnership

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.280
in the Elevate Policy Lab,

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:03.610
aimed at reducing depressive symptoms

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:05.910
and increasing social
and economic mobility

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:08.540
for low-income mothers.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:13.010
In addition, Nandini
has served on the board

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:16.820
of Yale's Emerging Majority
Students Association

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:18.660
and as a teaching fellow

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.300
for Health Equity &amp; Social Justice

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.763
and LGBTQ Population Health.

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:26.110
She looks forward to rejoining

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:29.660
the public health
workforce post-graduation,

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:32.430
with the ultimate goal
of pursuing a doctorate

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:35.340
in clinical or counseling psychology.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:38.260
It is a an honor and an absolute delight

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:42.840
to invite you to the podium, Nandini Deo,

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:46.692
our 2022 student speaker.

00:09:46.692 --> 00:09:49.692
(audience cheering)

00:10:02.968 --> 00:10:05.801
(Nandini squeals)

00:10:10.106 --> 00:10:13.050
(audience cheering)

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:15.833
- Thanks, friends. Hi, good afternoon.

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:18.953
I can't believe this
is actually happening.

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:21.330
Amazing.

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:23.370
So writing this graduation speech felt...

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:26.270
Actually, it felt a lot
like graduate school:

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:29.000
Me and my bestie, Google,
against the world.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.670
You can't exactly Google "how
to write a graduation speech

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:33.540
for a class that voluntarily
studied public health

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:36.690
amidst a global pandemic,"
but for what it's worth,

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:39.093
I'll have you know that I did try.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.970
Google said to find a powerful hook,

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:47.220
something compelling, profound even.

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:48.830
So I went to the next place

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:52.310
I knew wouldn't fail me: Academic Twitter.

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:55.040
As expected, I found
myself in a rabbit hole

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:57.670
where I understood about
30% of the vocabulary

00:10:57.670 --> 00:10:59.180
and even less of the theory.

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:01.370
And based on this excellent knowledge base

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:03.750
and a vague interpretation
of others' profundity,

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:06.440
here I am in front of you all.

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:08.440
The last speech I wrote began

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:09.900
with a quote from the Dalai Lama,

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:11.940
and this one's from an
organizational psychologist

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:13.210
with an impressive Twitter feed.

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:17.973
So academia has, simply
put, broadened my horizons.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:22.897
Adam Grant wrote, in
less than 280 characters,

00:11:22.897 --> 00:11:25.940
"Too many people spend their
lives being dutiful descendants

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:27.890
instead of good ancestors.

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:30.270
The responsibility of
each generation is not

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:31.710
to please their predecessors,

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:33.947
it's to improve things
for their offspring."

00:11:34.900 --> 00:11:36.830
This Tweet actually reminded
me of a conversation

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:37.990
I'd had with a supervisor

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:39.430
in my early years of public health,

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:40.470
who advised that our role

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:43.690
in this field was to water
a garden, consistently,

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:47.350
meticulously, whether or
not we'd ever see it bloom.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:48.980
It's taken me many more years,

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:50.850
and experiences with you all,

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:54.640
to realize that our garden is
literally the entire world,

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:57.690
and that watering it comes
in many different forms:

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:00.390
from improving water quality
to increasing hygiene

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:02.670
and sanitation in conflict zones,

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:05.490
modeling the impacts of
major health policies,

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:08.400
fundamentally restructuring
our health care systems,

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:10.290
designing sustainability interventions

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.180
to ameliorate climate change,

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:15.420
analyzing and triangulating
millions of data points

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.870
in an effort to reduce
transmission of disease,

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:19.650
and finding new and creative ways

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:22.410
to motivate belief and behavior change.

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:25.730
Now, more than ever, it is
clear that public health impacts

00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:29.130
every system on every
corner of this earth.

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:31.080
It's an exciting realization,

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:34.420
perhaps coupled with big fat overwhelm,

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:36.580
because our careers are
often a labor of love

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:38.410
with the central goal of improving life

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:40.530
for those who may come after us.

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:43.080
In a field so broad, ever-changing,

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.410
and, now, highly politicized,

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:48.420
what does it mean to
become a good ancestor?

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:49.500
I'm not gonna pretend like I know

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:50.700
the answer to this question

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:52.280
or that I'm even under the assumption

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:54.030
that there is one answer.

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:55.700
After all, one of the
many things we've learned

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:58.490
at our time here at YSPH is
that there are many truths,

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:01.540
and we will be tasked with
making room for them all.

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:04.290
This program did, however,
train many of us as researchers

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.100
who use observation and conversation

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:08.620
to make meaning and consider solutions.

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:10.640
So this my earnest attempt,

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:13.130
without sounding too much
like a live, laugh, love sign,

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:14.330
to give it my best shot.

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:19.490
I do believe that a good
ancestor is driven by hope.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:21.370
When I began this program two years ago,

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:23.530
my aunt, a fellow public
health practitioner,

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:24.647
sent me an article called

00:13:24.647 --> 00:13:28.260
"Hope, Cynicism, and the
Stories We Tell Ourselves."

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:29.797
The article astutely remarked,

00:13:29.797 --> 00:13:32.640
"Critical thinking
without hope is cynicism.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:35.920
Hope without critical
thinking is naivete."

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:37.700
This seemed, at the time, and even still,

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:40.650
the perfect description of
the public health conundrum:

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:42.810
the call to hope is
really a call to retain

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:45.440
a critical mind and a soft heart.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:48.310
A critical mind to question everything:

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:51.120
the methods, the systems,
the dissemination strategies,

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:52.760
the funding mechanisms, the theories,

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:55.430
the power differentials,
the service delivery.

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:58.200
A soft heart to remember
why we do the work:

00:13:58.200 --> 00:13:59.950
to see communities thrive,

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:02.750
to continue to believe
that change is possible,

00:14:02.750 --> 00:14:04.510
to give the gifts that we were given

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:05.910
and the gifts we've cultivated

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:09.570
to create a world that people
actually want to live in.

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:11.930
Here in public health, hope
isn't really an option,

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:13.400
it is a necessity.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:14.980
As practitioners of public health,

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:16.320
regardless of our concentration,

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.210
we witness, up close, the very worst

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:21.130
and the very best that
humanity has to offer.

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:23.270
This reminds me of a text
chain my roommates and I sent

00:14:23.270 --> 00:14:25.487
after opening one of our
readings last spring:

00:14:25.487 --> 00:14:28.397
"The Epidemiology of Human Suffering."

00:14:28.397 --> 00:14:31.580
"Amazing," we said. "What
a warm and fuzzy field."

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.480
Many of us have seen patients turned away

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.540
because of lack of documentation,

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:37.650
have contended with
harmful policies passed

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:39.930
after decades of health advocacy,

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:41.620
have witnessed public trust dwindle

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:43.830
throughout the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:46.410
thwarting infection reduction plans.

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:48.510
Still, we continue to find hope

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:51.000
through many different avenues.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:53.230
Hope comes in moments of curiosity,

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:55.300
in moments we feel
compelled to ask questions

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:58.700
about ourselves, others,
and the world around us.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:01.530
Curiosity cultivates hope
through refusal of stagnation.

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:03.410
It insists there must be more:

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:07.070
new options, solutions, considerations.

00:15:07.070 --> 00:15:10.237
I hope you hold on to the
power of asking, "But why?"

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:13.040
Hope comes in moments of love.

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:14.350
Before many of us knew each other,

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:16.600
we were just tiny boxes on Zoom screens,

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:18.620
smiling when our roommates or partners

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:21.150
or family members said
something from off-screen,

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:24.320
mouthing "thank you" to the
person who delivered us hot tea,

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:26.840
or laughing when our
pets entered our screen,

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:29.610
pausing our attention to text
our friends back mid-lecture,

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:32.640
and creating new relationships
through Zoom chat.

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:34.490
Love cultivates hope through remembering

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:37.380
that we are all whole
people with full lives.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:40.350
I hope you continue to foster
love in your everyday life,

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:43.783
so you can continue to extend
love to the people you serve.

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:47.260
Hope comes in moments of creativity

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:49.660
when we utilize a
combination of imagination

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:50.950
and evidence-based practice

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:53.380
or a new strategy or a novel method.

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:55.770
Creativity cultivates hope
through the recognition

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:57.480
that public health and medical systems

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:00.390
that we live within
were created by someone.

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:03.150
Why wouldn't we create the next iteration?

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:05.660
I hope you acknowledge
your power as an individual

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:07.460
who will shape the future of health.

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:12.560
Finally and, in my unsolicited
opinion, most importantly,

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:15.830
hope comes in moments of humor.

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:18.020
Humor, I think, is
humanity's most endearing

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:20.440
survival tactic against adversity.

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:21.820
We laugh about irony.

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:23.940
We laugh hysterically when sleep-deprived,

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:26.620
something I'm sure none
of us can relate to.

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:28.370
We laugh, filled with adrenaline,

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:31.290
when we exhibit bravery
to speak truth to power.

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:34.210
We laugh to connect with
others, to release tension,

00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:37.640
to try again and again and again.

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:41.610
I hope you find humor and
connection to keep you grounded.

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:44.750
Class of 2022, I don't
know exactly what it means

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:48.290
to be a good ancestor, but
I do know you are all it.

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:50.030
Your bright minds, your curiosity,

00:16:50.030 --> 00:16:52.310
your ability to hold
the worst and the best

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:54.940
the world has to offer,
your wit and your humor,

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:57.730
your critical minds, and your soft hearts.

00:16:57.730 --> 00:16:59.250
We're entering the field of public health

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:02.600
at a time in history when our
hope is so desperately needed.

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:05.000
And I do believe that
we have some despair.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.110
I can't wait to see you out
there making your corner

00:17:07.110 --> 00:17:09.270
of the world a better place to live in.

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:10.670
Congratulations.

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:13.670
(audience cheering)

00:17:30.119 --> 00:17:32.870
- Nandini, thank you very
much for those words.

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:33.943
We're very grateful.

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:37.510
It's now my privilege to
introduce our distinguished

00:17:37.510 --> 00:17:41.510
2022 Commencement
speaker, Dr. Heidi Larson.

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:43.290
She has to present to us

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:46.260
from the World Economic
Forum in Davos, Switzerland,

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:49.980
because she was previously
committed to that event.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:52.820
Professor Heidi Larson
is the founding director

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:55.490
of the Vaccine Confidence Project

00:17:55.490 --> 00:17:59.830
and is Professor of Anthropology,
Risk and Decision Science

00:17:59.830 --> 00:18:02.740
at the London School of
Hygiene &amp; Tropical Medicine.

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:05.040
She also holds a clinical professorship

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:06.760
at the University of Washington

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:08.300
and a guest professorship

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:11.583
at the University of Antwerp in Leuven.

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:16.730
Her research focuses on the analysis

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:18.830
of social and political factors

00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:21.600
and managing risk and rumors,

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:24.600
including digitally-enabled disinformation

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:28.550
that can affect uptake
of health interventions.

00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:31.230
Her current research
is particularly focused

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:35.080
on issues around public
cooperation during emergencies,

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:37.800
disease outbreak, natural disasters,

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:39.963
terrorism, and conflict.

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:42.780
Professor Larson previously headed

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:47.670
Global Immunization Communication
at UNICEF, chaired Gavi's,

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:49.230
that's the Global Alliance

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:53.230
for Vaccines and Immunization
advocacy taskforce,

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:55.650
and served on the World
Health Organization,

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:59.023
SAGE working group, on vaccine hesitancy.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:01.337
She is author of a book called

00:19:01.337 --> 00:19:04.310
"Stuck: How Vaccine Rumors Start

00:19:04.310 --> 00:19:06.120
and Why They Don't Go Away,"

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:09.480
published in 2020 by
Oxford University Press.

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:11.680
She's published widely on the drivers

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:15.240
and enablers of vaccine confidence.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:18.610
In 2021, she was awarded
the Edinburgh Medal,

00:19:18.610 --> 00:19:20.540
in recognition of the role she has played

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:22.920
in recognizing importance of popular

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:26.720
and widespread
misunderstandings of vaccines.

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:29.620
BBC also named Professor Larson

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:32.150
as one of the hundred
most influential women

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:34.373
in the world in 2021.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:37.640
Dr. Larson joins us via cyberspace

00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:40.120
to give her 2022 Commencement address

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:44.440
to the MPH class of 2022.

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:47.930
Graduates, faculty, staff,
families, and friends,

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:51.523
it's a pleasure to introduce
my friend, Dr. Heidi Larson.

00:19:51.523 --> 00:19:54.690
(audience applauding)

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:00.820
- Congratulations to all of you.

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:03.110
And I'm really sorry not to be with you

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:06.583
in-person in New Haven,
which I would've loved to do.

00:20:07.687 --> 00:20:09.710
But I'm really grateful
for the opportunity

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:11.850
to prerecord this address.

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:14.840
And I chose to record it
in the science gallery

00:20:14.840 --> 00:20:17.593
here in Bengaluru, India,

00:20:17.593 --> 00:20:22.140
where I've been collaborating
in an exhibit on contagion,

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:24.020
and thought it was an appropriate setting

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:25.590
to be addressing you,

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:27.620
given their inspirational efforts

00:20:27.620 --> 00:20:30.930
at making science more
engaging and accessible,

00:20:30.930 --> 00:20:33.363
which is foundational to public health.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:36.620
Well, what an important day this is.

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:40.020
It's a milestone in your
public health careers.

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:41.550
It's actually the beginning,

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:44.900
the commencement of your learning,

00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:46.570
a different kind of learning,

00:20:46.570 --> 00:20:49.800
a real-world learning,
and your leadership.

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:53.210
You are some of the top public health

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:55.480
emerging leaders in the world,

00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:57.220
and we're all counting on you,

00:20:57.220 --> 00:21:01.120
particularly after this
challenging couple years.

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:04.400
You couldn't have had
a more meaningful time

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:06.510
to earn your public health degree

00:21:07.592 --> 00:21:11.170
than in the context of
this current pandemic.

00:21:11.170 --> 00:21:14.070
You've also seen the real-world reality

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:17.490
of public health outside
of your classrooms,

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:21.790
the swirl of politics,
of publics, and science,

00:21:21.790 --> 00:21:24.880
all competing, sometimes,
and not cooperating,

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:27.963
while trying to cooperate for attention.

00:21:28.880 --> 00:21:32.770
Thankfully, not all public
health work is in crises,

00:21:32.770 --> 00:21:36.350
and the best of public
health work helps to mitigate

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:39.810
and prevent extreme health outcomes

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:44.433
in the context of crisis and
other settings and emergencies.

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:49.650
As we slowly and
hopefully exit from COVID,

00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:53.610
all eyes will be on building
preparedness and resilience

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:56.120
in the face of the next challenge,

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:58.630
whether it's a serious disease outbreak,

00:21:58.630 --> 00:22:01.760
or other events such as natural disasters

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:04.780
with their health consequences.

00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:07.630
We'll also need to address
the many health needs

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:10.630
that were overlooked or neglected

00:22:10.630 --> 00:22:14.700
because we had all eyes on COVID
in these past couple years.

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:17.460
Some of these issues,
like even mental health,

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:22.010
which were exacerbated,
made worse during COVID.

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:24.670
I read a recent report
that the pandemic led

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:28.180
to an additional 53 million cases

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:31.400
of depression in the world since COVID.

00:22:31.400 --> 00:22:33.483
We have a lot of work to do.

00:22:34.870 --> 00:22:38.040
As you choose your path after graduation,

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:41.200
because public health embraces
so many different paths

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:42.690
and the wonder of public health,

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:45.990
and what I've loved being
an anthropologist working

00:22:45.990 --> 00:22:48.260
in public health is the diversity

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:49.920
of people I've worked with:

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:53.040
my research group, the
Vaccine Confidence Project.

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:56.240
We have computer scientists,
mathematical modelers,

00:22:56.240 --> 00:23:00.010
psychiatrists, political scientists,

00:23:00.010 --> 00:23:02.410
epidemiologists, vaccinologists.

00:23:02.410 --> 00:23:05.750
And it's the wonder of bringing
these disciplines together

00:23:05.750 --> 00:23:09.423
to ensure more robust public health.

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:13.717
The biggest message I can
give you when I thought about,

00:23:13.717 --> 00:23:17.830
"What can I tell such a
brilliant group of graduates

00:23:17.830 --> 00:23:20.550
after everything they've already learned?"

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:22.080
and one of the key messages

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:25.830
I thought was be open, listen carefully.

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:28.650
This is the beginning of learning.

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:31.270
Everyone around you as you enter

00:23:31.270 --> 00:23:35.720
into your chosen role/setting
that you work in,

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:37.810
you'll be around people who, each of them,

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:40.750
has had a different experience
these last few years.

00:23:40.750 --> 00:23:43.500
Different challenges at many levels.

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:46.130
Building the trust of those you work with

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:49.920
in your new setting will be
crucial to building trust,

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:54.090
listening in even small ways
to what they've been through

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:57.223
as a gesture of caring about the others.

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:01.830
Public trust has been
a key issue, a lever,

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:04.500
a key lever of public cooperation

00:24:05.990 --> 00:24:08.020
around the multiple restrictions

00:24:08.020 --> 00:24:10.480
and challenges we've had during COVID,

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:14.090
imposed on people for "the greater good."

00:24:14.090 --> 00:24:16.480
Well, what we, as public health community,

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:19.890
feel is the greater good
wasn't necessarily felt

00:24:19.890 --> 00:24:24.060
to be the greater good locally
or for some populations.

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:27.693
This has been a challenge and
a relationship-building time.

00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:30.580
Countries where there was higher trust

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:33.070
in government fared must better than those

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:36.600
who didn't have as much
trust in government.

00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:37.790
We learned, for instance,

00:24:37.790 --> 00:24:40.670
that our global vaccine
confidence research

00:24:40.670 --> 00:24:42.370
that the biggest predictor

00:24:42.370 --> 00:24:46.120
of COVID vaccine willingness
was actually the perception

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:48.933
that government was
handling the pandemic well.

00:24:49.930 --> 00:24:51.920
The more confidence in the government

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:54.460
and how they were handling the pandemic,

00:24:54.460 --> 00:24:57.480
the more willingness to accept a vaccine

00:24:57.480 --> 00:25:00.280
that was administered, regulated,

00:25:00.280 --> 00:25:02.463
and, sometimes, required by government.

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:06.530
I care passionately about public trust.

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:09.040
And I'm hugely concerned about the recent

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:12.720
World Economic Forum risk report

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:16.780
which rated social risk cohesion erosion,

00:25:16.780 --> 00:25:20.140
the ability of society to cooperate,

00:25:20.140 --> 00:25:22.300
as the global risk that worsened

00:25:22.300 --> 00:25:26.650
the most dramatically during
COVID, during the pandemic.

00:25:26.650 --> 00:25:29.530
We would have thought, we
could have thought and hoped,

00:25:29.530 --> 00:25:32.580
that this would have
been a time to embellish

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:36.340
and strengthen social cohesion,
but, for whatever reason,

00:25:36.340 --> 00:25:39.740
some of which were very
different in different settings,

00:25:39.740 --> 00:25:44.020
social cohesion and cooperation
didn't fare as well,

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:46.510
but they're foundational to public health.

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:49.840
And this will be absolutely
crucial for you to consider

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:52.250
and think about how you can contribute

00:25:52.250 --> 00:25:54.963
to rebuilding it in your work ahead.

00:25:56.050 --> 00:26:00.370
My second message, besides
be open, listen carefully,

00:26:00.370 --> 00:26:02.850
is be ready for surprises.

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:06.290
One of the biggest learnings
of the pandemic has been

00:26:06.290 --> 00:26:08.690
the importance of being responsive

00:26:08.690 --> 00:26:12.080
to changing circumstances, uncertainty,

00:26:12.080 --> 00:26:14.960
even changing science, evolving science.

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:18.030
That's been one of the biggest
challenges of the pandemic,

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:20.080
keeping the public on board

00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:22.510
while the information was changing,

00:26:22.510 --> 00:26:26.010
weighing what was the
best available evidence

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:29.420
to make the best decisions,

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:32.810
recognizing that they may change.

00:26:32.810 --> 00:26:34.930
This is not the stuff of textbooks,

00:26:34.930 --> 00:26:38.570
of linear thinking about
evidence to policy.

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:42.210
Multiple types of evidence
and situational awareness

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:44.820
weigh into public health decisions.

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:46.850
Knowing that, again, they may need

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:49.323
to change as situations evolve.

00:26:50.690 --> 00:26:54.280
The world did not stand
together in a kumbaya moment

00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:55.893
to get us through this crisis.

00:26:57.240 --> 00:26:59.963
The best successes when people ask me,

00:27:00.887 --> 00:27:03.200
"Where did you see successes?"

00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:06.440
the successes I saw were hyper local.

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:08.270
They were local communities.

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:12.350
They were giving and caring
and mutual trust settings

00:27:12.350 --> 00:27:14.560
that people pulled together

00:27:14.560 --> 00:27:17.450
and understood each other's needs.

00:27:17.450 --> 00:27:21.730
And, also, the local health
institutions, local governments,

00:27:21.730 --> 00:27:26.270
local schools understood
their local community.

00:27:26.270 --> 00:27:29.153
Local and locality mattered,

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:32.590
because, sometimes, national leaders,

00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:35.160
global leaders that we would have expected

00:27:35.160 --> 00:27:38.220
to line up together, weren't doing that,

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:41.120
as many pandemic preparedness reports

00:27:41.120 --> 00:27:46.120
and commissions had anticipated,
assumed, or at least hoped.

00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:50.810
Some leaders, as you know, misled publics

00:27:50.810 --> 00:27:55.120
away from the science, down
the rabbit hole of conspiracy.

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:57.880
In those settings, when you can't have

00:27:57.880 --> 00:28:00.470
that kind of confidence
in a national leader,

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:03.880
localness and local leadership, mayors,

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:07.290
and others in local settings
matter tremendously,

00:28:07.290 --> 00:28:10.880
religious leaders, others,
who the public trust

00:28:10.880 --> 00:28:13.363
when there is so much uncertainty.

00:28:14.620 --> 00:28:16.290
What has been particularly striking

00:28:16.290 --> 00:28:17.890
and concerning about the nature

00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:22.610
and scope of conspiracy,
rumors, and misinformation,

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:25.410
a topic I've spent a
tremendous amount of time

00:28:25.410 --> 00:28:29.130
with my research group on
these past couple years,

00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:32.060
is the level of aggression
and anger behind it.

00:28:32.060 --> 00:28:36.330
There's been tremendous
emotion during this pandemic.

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:38.910
And, somehow, moving forward,

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:40.560
we're going to need to calm

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:44.450
and rebuild a more hopeful, optimistic,

00:28:44.450 --> 00:28:47.203
rather than angry and anxious public.

00:28:48.130 --> 00:28:49.840
It's not gonna be easy,

00:28:49.840 --> 00:28:53.220
but I think we're all
counting on all of you

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:56.100
and your colleagues in other universities

00:28:57.180 --> 00:28:58.670
to work towards this,

00:28:58.670 --> 00:29:02.480
to be good listeners and trust-builders,

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:05.640
because we have a pretty
fractured environment,

00:29:05.640 --> 00:29:06.910
but not everywhere.

00:29:06.910 --> 00:29:08.600
We need to build on the hope,

00:29:08.600 --> 00:29:12.023
build on the optimism,
build on the local trust.

00:29:12.941 --> 00:29:15.120
In the beginning of the pandemic,

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:19.110
which was an early signal that
we had some challenges ahead,

00:29:19.110 --> 00:29:23.780
I remember nearly 60 5G
towers were set on fire.

00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:28.720
Others vandalized, people
working there were harassed,

00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:31.050
and I was really startled

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:33.910
at how extreme their reactions were.

00:29:33.910 --> 00:29:36.920
And some of it was against people

00:29:36.920 --> 00:29:39.290
not wanting to be told what to do,

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:42.033
how to run their life and where.

00:29:43.130 --> 00:29:45.000
As vaccines became available

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:49.290
and increasingly required in
many settings, as you know,

00:29:49.290 --> 00:29:52.180
they, too, have been a
trigger of anger and anxiety

00:29:52.180 --> 00:29:55.100
for some populations, for some people.

00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:58.670
Some of it was more anxiety
than anger. It's uncertain.

00:29:58.670 --> 00:30:01.140
It was so new. It was so quick.

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:02.980
And it amplified.

00:30:02.980 --> 00:30:05.590
There was already underlying trends

00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:08.400
of vaccine hesitancy around the world.

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:12.230
But where we did well, it was
a signal of trust-building.

00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:13.800
I've always been struck.

00:30:13.800 --> 00:30:16.020
I never expected to be spending

00:30:16.020 --> 00:30:19.320
nearly 20 years of my
life working in the field

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:22.960
of vaccine confidence after
many years working in AIDS,

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:24.880
which was very much more bottom-up

00:30:24.880 --> 00:30:28.003
than top-down as a pandemic response.

00:30:31.550 --> 00:30:34.600
Vaccine confidence has such a window

00:30:34.600 --> 00:30:37.273
on people's ability to cooperate.

00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:43.430
The last message, which
I've already suggested

00:30:43.430 --> 00:30:48.430
and referred to, is to never
forget the emotions of publics.

00:30:48.490 --> 00:30:51.800
And they're more heightened than ever.

00:30:51.800 --> 00:30:53.900
Publics are hugely heterogeneous.

00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:55.950
There's no one public.

00:30:55.950 --> 00:30:58.543
And in that sense, no one public health.

00:30:59.684 --> 00:31:03.160
They're hugely heterogeneous,
with multiple histories,

00:31:03.160 --> 00:31:06.400
beliefs, political stances, and emotions,

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:10.420
and all of these factors have
influenced their willingness,

00:31:10.420 --> 00:31:14.143
or not, to cooperate for the greater good.

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:18.880
All of these factors
matter as we work together

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:21.343
for public health and better health.

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:26.670
Your effectiveness as
public health practitioners

00:31:26.670 --> 00:31:29.190
will be determined by your alertness

00:31:29.190 --> 00:31:32.290
and sensitivity to these human factors.

00:31:32.290 --> 00:31:35.250
We have to embrace these factors

00:31:35.250 --> 00:31:39.033
without losing the foundational
science behind them.

00:31:40.130 --> 00:31:42.660
While we can think of universal principles

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:46.190
for public health, and
perhaps shared goals,

00:31:46.190 --> 00:31:49.820
there will be varied
journeys to get there.

00:31:49.820 --> 00:31:53.450
There is no one universal best practice.

00:31:53.450 --> 00:31:56.783
There are many local best practices.

00:31:58.220 --> 00:32:01.610
Be open, listen carefully.

00:32:01.610 --> 00:32:06.200
Be open, listen carefully
as you start your journey

00:32:06.200 --> 00:32:09.310
and don't, don't stop listening.

00:32:09.310 --> 00:32:13.520
It's the most important
thing that I can think of.

00:32:13.520 --> 00:32:17.270
Each time you listen, it
is a gesture of caring

00:32:17.270 --> 00:32:21.640
about what the other
person or people think.

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:24.790
It's a trust builder. Thank you.

00:32:24.790 --> 00:32:27.287
And, again, congratulations.

00:32:27.287 --> 00:32:30.454
(audience applauding)

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:39.270
- Thank you, Professor Larson.

00:32:39.270 --> 00:32:41.720
If you didn't detect a British accent,

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:44.583
it's because Professor
Larson is from Connecticut.

