00:45:54.830 --> 00:45:59.053
Tom's score was the highest
score ever achieved by a-

00:46:00.012 --> 00:46:03.012
(audience cheering)

00:46:09.906 --> 00:46:12.014
(audience member shouting)

00:46:12.014 --> 00:46:14.514
(Sten laughs)

00:46:15.850 --> 00:46:17.090
Highest score ever achieved

00:46:17.090 --> 00:46:19.380
by a Biostats teaching team member,

00:46:19.380 --> 00:46:22.239
and nearly double that of
the course instructor."

00:46:22.239 --> 00:46:23.660
(audience laughing)

00:46:23.660 --> 00:46:27.430
When prompted for criticisms
of Tom's teaching,

00:46:27.430 --> 00:46:30.260
the students had two suggestions for Tom:

00:46:30.260 --> 00:46:32.730
pick a different hour
to host your section,

00:46:32.730 --> 00:46:35.100
and number two, don't tap the microphone

00:46:35.100 --> 00:46:36.193
to test the room mic.

00:46:37.108 --> 00:46:39.160
Those were the two
suggestions that he got.

00:46:39.160 --> 00:46:40.720
Those were the only two.

00:46:40.720 --> 00:46:42.500
Tom was beloved, as you can tell,

00:46:42.500 --> 00:46:45.400
and these critiques are
as harsh as it gets.

00:46:45.400 --> 00:46:47.830
Students underscored that
"he's patient, understanding,

00:46:47.830 --> 00:46:49.820
and determined to ensure
that students leave

00:46:49.820 --> 00:46:51.730
his section feeling confident

00:46:51.730 --> 00:46:55.160
about what they have
just learned in EPH 505."

00:46:55.160 --> 00:46:57.640
Students also expressed
that "Tom is so awesome,

00:46:57.640 --> 00:47:00.137
so approachable, so
knowledgeable, and flexible."

00:47:01.390 --> 00:47:02.890
People say that about me, don't they?

00:47:02.890 --> 00:47:04.150
No, no, no.
(audience laughing)

00:47:04.150 --> 00:47:06.697
No, I guess, in fact, they don't.

00:47:06.697 --> 00:47:09.640
"He was super helpful,
especially during lab,

00:47:09.640 --> 00:47:12.320
when many of us are learning
to code for the first time,

00:47:12.320 --> 00:47:14.530
and I really appreciated that he sent kind

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:17.750
and supportive notes to us
throughout the semester."

00:47:17.750 --> 00:47:20.955
Tom, finally, please come
to receive your award.

00:47:20.955 --> 00:47:23.955
(audience cheering)

00:47:47.860 --> 00:47:50.293
All right. We now turn to the faculty.

00:47:51.160 --> 00:47:56.010
The YSPH Distinguished Student
Mentoring Award recognizes

00:47:56.010 --> 00:48:00.080
a faculty member as a leader
in shaping the next generation

00:48:00.080 --> 00:48:01.890
of public health professionals

00:48:01.890 --> 00:48:04.200
and one who serves as a role model,

00:48:04.200 --> 00:48:05.940
conveys passion for public health,

00:48:05.940 --> 00:48:08.180
has clear and high expectations,

00:48:08.180 --> 00:48:11.200
encourages students to
achieve their full potential,

00:48:11.200 --> 00:48:13.920
is sensitive to student
needs and concerns,

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:16.880
and makes extraordinary
efforts to help their students.

00:48:16.880 --> 00:48:19.670
Candidates for this award are nominated

00:48:19.670 --> 00:48:22.190
by graduating students and
are selected by a committee

00:48:22.190 --> 00:48:24.770
of both students and faculty.

00:48:24.770 --> 00:48:27.040
This year, the 2022 recipient

00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:29.350
of the Distinguished Student Mentor Award

00:48:29.350 --> 00:48:31.676
is Dr. Ijeoma Opara.

00:48:31.676 --> 00:48:34.676
(audience cheering)

00:48:50.855 --> 00:48:53.750
(audience member shouting)

00:48:53.750 --> 00:48:57.280
Dr. Opara, who's been here about a year,

00:48:57.280 --> 00:48:58.570
is an assistant professor

00:48:58.570 --> 00:49:00.610
in the Department of Social
&amp; Behavioral Sciences

00:49:00.610 --> 00:49:02.490
at our School of Public Health.

00:49:02.490 --> 00:49:04.160
She is the founder and director

00:49:04.160 --> 00:49:07.430
of the Substance Abuse
&amp; Sexual Health Lab,

00:49:07.430 --> 00:49:12.040
www.oparalab.org.

00:49:12.040 --> 00:49:16.600
Her research interests
focus on HIV/AIDS, STIs,

00:49:16.600 --> 00:49:20.320
and substance use
prevention for urban youth,

00:49:20.320 --> 00:49:22.610
including racial and gender-specific

00:49:22.610 --> 00:49:25.640
prevention interventions for Black girls,

00:49:25.640 --> 00:49:30.170
and community-based participatory
research with urban youth.

00:49:30.170 --> 00:49:34.430
Dr. Opara was praised by students
for being an inspiration.

00:49:34.430 --> 00:49:37.170
They wrote, "In order to
be an inspiring mentor,

00:49:37.170 --> 00:49:40.450
a professor must possess
the following qualities:

00:49:40.450 --> 00:49:43.000
ability to provoke original ideas,

00:49:43.000 --> 00:49:46.560
dedication to their work and
their students, compassion,

00:49:46.560 --> 00:49:49.400
and ability to instill
passion in their students.

00:49:49.400 --> 00:49:52.930
Dr. Opara exemplifies
all of these qualities."

00:49:52.930 --> 00:49:57.440
Students also wrote,
"Dr. Opara is the epitome

00:49:57.440 --> 00:50:01.230
of the positive impact
exceptional mentors can have

00:50:01.230 --> 00:50:04.700
on minority students
at elite institutions."

00:50:04.700 --> 00:50:07.489
Ijeoma, my friend, please
come to receive your award.

00:50:07.489 --> 00:50:10.489
(audience cheering)

00:50:22.944 --> 00:50:26.277
(Sten faintly speaking)

00:50:32.990 --> 00:50:35.220
The Distinguished Teaching Award,

00:50:35.220 --> 00:50:37.580
also called Teacher of the Year,

00:50:37.580 --> 00:50:39.650
is the school's highest
honor for its faculty,

00:50:39.650 --> 00:50:42.990
bestowed by its graduating students.

00:50:42.990 --> 00:50:46.230
Quality teaching is one of
the most important influences

00:50:46.230 --> 00:50:48.110
on a student's experience at Yale,

00:50:48.110 --> 00:50:52.320
and this award recognizes
the recipient's leading role

00:50:52.320 --> 00:50:53.940
in shaping the next generation

00:50:53.940 --> 00:50:56.130
of public health professionals.

00:50:56.130 --> 00:50:59.710
The 2022 recipient of the
Distinguished Teaching Award

00:50:59.710 --> 00:51:01.710
is Professor David Paltiel,

00:51:01.710 --> 00:51:04.494
in the Department of Health
Policy and Management.

00:51:04.494 --> 00:51:07.494
(audience cheering)

00:51:09.565 --> 00:51:12.065
(Sten laughs)

00:51:14.800 --> 00:51:16.910
The objective that guides Dr. Paltiel's

00:51:16.910 --> 00:51:20.220
scholarly activities is to
promote a reasoned approach

00:51:20.220 --> 00:51:23.610
to decision-making and resource allocation

00:51:23.610 --> 00:51:25.620
in public health and medicine.

00:51:25.620 --> 00:51:28.100
Trained in the field
of operations research,

00:51:28.100 --> 00:51:31.543
Dr. Paltiel designs and
implements policy models

00:51:31.543 --> 00:51:35.520
and cost-effectiveness analyses.

00:51:35.520 --> 00:51:39.370
He has a special interest
and expertise in HIV/AIDS

00:51:39.370 --> 00:51:42.270
and has published broadly
on the cost-effectiveness

00:51:42.270 --> 00:51:44.920
of testing, prevention,
treatment, and care,

00:51:44.920 --> 00:51:47.660
both in the United States
and around the world.

00:51:47.660 --> 00:51:49.270
David is praised by his students

00:51:49.270 --> 00:51:51.577
for being excited by, quote,

00:51:51.577 --> 00:51:54.640
"every student question,
answering them with eagerness

00:51:54.640 --> 00:51:57.060
and appreciation for students' curiosity

00:51:57.060 --> 00:51:59.420
and creative, critical thinking.

00:51:59.420 --> 00:52:01.140
He created a sense of community

00:52:01.140 --> 00:52:04.940
among his students and TFs
by celebrating our growth

00:52:04.940 --> 00:52:08.470
and recognizing the specific
contributions people made

00:52:08.470 --> 00:52:10.830
to the group's learning."

00:52:10.830 --> 00:52:12.640
I am so fortunate to have known

00:52:12.640 --> 00:52:14.750
and been influenced by Dr. Paltiel

00:52:14.750 --> 00:52:16.560
for all of over a quarter century.

00:52:16.560 --> 00:52:19.923
David, please come forward
and receive your award.

00:52:19.923 --> 00:52:22.923
(audience cheering)

00:52:39.462 --> 00:52:42.540
I forgot to mention David's
important work in COVID-19.

00:52:42.540 --> 00:52:45.850
He pivoted his experience immediately

00:52:45.850 --> 00:52:50.850
and made nationally influential
contributions in the area,

00:52:51.770 --> 00:52:53.343
but this is all for teaching.

00:52:55.703 --> 00:52:57.030
This is not for that.

00:52:57.030 --> 00:53:01.160
So, Tom, Ijeoma, and David
will have their names inscribed

00:53:01.160 --> 00:53:04.420
on the Awards Wall on the
first floor teaching area

00:53:04.420 --> 00:53:05.953
in 60 College Street.

00:53:07.266 --> 00:53:10.433
(audience applauding)

00:53:16.920 --> 00:53:18.530
Next, we would like to acknowledge

00:53:18.530 --> 00:53:20.910
our new faculty Research Awards.

00:53:20.910 --> 00:53:23.710
Just initiated in recent years

00:53:23.710 --> 00:53:27.330
by our associate dean, Melinda Irwin.

00:53:27.330 --> 00:53:29.940
Our new faculty Research Awards recognize

00:53:31.770 --> 00:53:34.680
distinguished peer-reviewed manuscripts.

00:53:34.680 --> 00:53:38.020
The research awardees were
already highlighted in detail

00:53:38.020 --> 00:53:41.350
at our YSPH faculty
meeting earlier this month

00:53:41.350 --> 00:53:43.070
to highlight the public health impact

00:53:43.070 --> 00:53:44.750
of their impressive research.

00:53:44.750 --> 00:53:48.950
However, I'd like to share
the awards with you briefly.

00:53:48.950 --> 00:53:52.560
The Early Career Investigator
Research Award is given

00:53:52.560 --> 00:53:56.210
to a YSPH faculty appointment
assistant professor

00:53:56.210 --> 00:53:58.320
or associate research scientist,

00:53:58.320 --> 00:54:00.080
who is either the first or last author

00:54:00.080 --> 00:54:02.300
on a given published manuscript.

00:54:02.300 --> 00:54:04.170
And we had a tie for this award.

00:54:04.170 --> 00:54:08.017
Congratulations to Dr. Kai
Chen for his paper entitled

00:54:08.017 --> 00:54:11.170
"Role of meteorological
factors in the transmission

00:54:11.170 --> 00:54:13.960
of SARS-CoV-2 in the United States."

00:54:13.960 --> 00:54:16.990
He published this in
"Nature Communications,"

00:54:16.990 --> 00:54:21.990
and to Dr. Fan Li in
Department of Biostatistics

00:54:22.470 --> 00:54:26.470
for his paper titled
"Sample size considerations

00:54:26.470 --> 00:54:29.390
for stepped wedge designs
with subclusters,"

00:54:29.390 --> 00:54:31.360
published in "Biometrics."

00:54:31.360 --> 00:54:34.160
Next is the Investigator Research Award,

00:54:34.160 --> 00:54:37.090
which is presented to a
YSPH primary faculty member

00:54:37.090 --> 00:54:40.460
of any track or rank who
was first or last author

00:54:40.460 --> 00:54:42.140
on the published manuscript.

00:54:42.140 --> 00:54:45.767
Congratulations to Professor
Becca Levy on the paper,

00:54:45.767 --> 00:54:49.930
"Impact of Media-Based Negative
and Positive Age Stereotypes

00:54:49.930 --> 00:54:52.920
on Older Individuals' Mental Health,"

00:54:52.920 --> 00:54:56.330
published in "The Journals
of Gerontology: Series B."

00:54:56.330 --> 00:55:00.427
And to Dr. Michaela Dinan on her paper

00:55:00.427 --> 00:55:03.520
"Analysis of Sociodemographic, Clinical,

00:55:03.520 --> 00:55:07.380
and Genomic Factors Associated
with Breast Cancer Mortality

00:55:07.380 --> 00:55:09.770
in the Linked Surveillance, Epidemiology,

00:55:09.770 --> 00:55:13.060
and End Results and Medicare Database,"

00:55:13.060 --> 00:55:15.860
published in "JAMA Network Open."

00:55:15.860 --> 00:55:19.990
Our next award is the YSPH
COVID-19 Research Award,

00:55:19.990 --> 00:55:24.460
granted to a YSPH faculty
member or group of YSPH faculty

00:55:24.460 --> 00:55:26.020
whose original research published

00:55:26.020 --> 00:55:29.330
in 2021 focused on COVID-19.

00:55:29.330 --> 00:55:30.750
Congratulations to...

00:55:30.750 --> 00:55:31.890
Are you ready?

00:55:31.890 --> 00:55:35.107
David Paltiel and Jason
Schwartz, on their paper,

00:55:35.107 --> 00:55:38.200
"Assessing COVID-19 Prevention Strategies

00:55:38.200 --> 00:55:39.560
to Permit the Safe Opening

00:55:39.560 --> 00:55:43.050
of Residential Colleges in Fall 2021,"

00:55:43.050 --> 00:55:45.290
published in "Annals of Internal Medicine"

00:55:45.290 --> 00:55:48.630
and recognized as one of
the highest impact papers

00:55:48.630 --> 00:55:50.720
in that journal that year,

00:55:50.720 --> 00:55:54.520
and Dr. Chantal Vogels,
Professor Albert Ko,

00:55:54.520 --> 00:55:57.410
Professor Saad Omer,
and Dr. Nate Grubaugh,

00:55:57.410 --> 00:56:01.210
on their paper the "Impact of circulating

00:56:01.210 --> 00:56:06.180
SARS-CoV-2 variants on mRNA
vaccine-induced immunity,"

00:56:06.180 --> 00:56:08.090
published in "Nature."

00:56:08.090 --> 00:56:12.310
Our next award is the YSPH
Health Equity Research Award,

00:56:12.310 --> 00:56:14.790
granted to a YSPH primary faculty member's

00:56:14.790 --> 00:56:17.180
exceptional health equity-related

00:56:17.180 --> 00:56:20.560
original research published in 2021.

00:56:20.560 --> 00:56:25.047
Congratulations to Dr.
Yusuf Ransome on his paper,

00:56:25.047 --> 00:56:27.230
"Neighborhood Social Cohesion

00:56:27.230 --> 00:56:31.470
and Inequalities in
COVID-19 Diagnosis Rates

00:56:31.470 --> 00:56:36.470
by Area-Level Black/African
American Racial Composition,"

00:56:36.490 --> 00:56:39.620
published in the "Journal
of Urban Health,"

00:56:39.620 --> 00:56:42.817
and Dr. Terika McCall on her paper,

00:56:42.817 --> 00:56:44.580
"Development of a Mobile App

00:56:44.580 --> 00:56:47.790
to Support Self-management
of Anxiety and Depression

00:56:47.790 --> 00:56:51.030
in African American
Women: Usability Study,"

00:56:51.030 --> 00:56:55.260
published in "JMIR Formative Research."

00:56:55.260 --> 00:56:58.740
The Team Research Award is
our final research prize,

00:56:58.740 --> 00:57:00.560
granted to a YSPH team,

00:57:00.560 --> 00:57:03.150
two or more primary faculty members,

00:57:03.150 --> 00:57:06.370
with one faculty member either
being first or last author.

00:57:06.370 --> 00:57:10.040
Congratulations to Hannah
Ehrlich, Amy Bei, Dan Weinberger,

00:57:10.040 --> 00:57:14.487
Josh Warren, and Sunhil
Parikh on their paper entitled

00:57:14.487 --> 00:57:16.550
"Mapping partner drug resistance

00:57:16.550 --> 00:57:20.710
to guide antimalarial
combination therapy policies

00:57:20.710 --> 00:57:22.440
in sub-Saharan Africa,"

00:57:22.440 --> 00:57:23.870
published in the "Proceedings

00:57:23.870 --> 00:57:26.410
of National Academies of Sciences,"

00:57:26.410 --> 00:57:30.950
and Tyler Shelby, Xin
Zhou, Donna Spiegelman,

00:57:30.950 --> 00:57:34.940
Lauretta Grau, Linda
Niccolai, and Luke Davis

00:57:34.940 --> 00:57:36.527
on their paper entitled

00:57:36.527 --> 00:57:39.680
"Lessons Learned From
COVID-19 Contact Tracing

00:57:39.680 --> 00:57:41.590
During a Public Health Emergency:

00:57:41.590 --> 00:57:43.980
A Prospective Implementation Study,"

00:57:43.980 --> 00:57:46.380
published in "Frontiers in Public Health."

00:57:46.380 --> 00:57:48.560
Let's have a round of applause

00:57:48.560 --> 00:57:50.584
for all of these
outstanding investigators.

00:57:50.584 --> 00:57:53.751
(audience applauding)

00:58:01.520 --> 00:58:03.820
Now is the moment you've
all been waiting for.

00:58:03.820 --> 00:58:07.260
We take time to honor
all of our MPH graduates.

00:58:07.260 --> 00:58:08.730
Can our graduates please follow

00:58:08.730 --> 00:58:11.450
